TITLE='Authentic title "8 Things That Are a Complete WASTE of Your Money" is a numbered-list warning frame. The script delivers eight items (warranties, subscriptions, overdraft fees, lottery, card interest, sports betting, investment fees, car debt) with a running composite household tally, so the promise is kept.'
THUMB='Registered maxresdefault 1280x720 (HTTP 200, real image). White background: the recurring cartoon man, angry, pointing at a trash can; "STOP BUYING THESE!" in heavy black capitals; the can holds a Starbucks-branded cup, an iPhone, a red shopping bag, sneakers, a T-shirt, a game controller and a Bitcoin coin, with a red X. At 320x180 the words and objects read clearly. None of the pictured items is among the script\'s eight (the script explicitly says the fix "isn\'t giving up your coffee"); the packaging promises consumer-goods frugality while the video covers fees, debt and gambling.'
SCRIPT='A single-narrator narrative listicle with two framing devices (two tradesmen with equal incomes, and a composite couple, Dileia and Reuben, on $96,000): eight items, each with a mechanism and a dollar figure, a running tally, a $10,000-a-year total compounded to about $946,000, an emotional passage, a regulatory-rollback reframe (overdraft cap, click-to-cancel, late-fee cap), a faceless-YouTube community promotion, and a callback close. Regulatory history is accurate; several calculations are not: the fee-drag gap uses a 20-year figure for 30 years, the tally sums to $8,215 as spoken, and the minimum-payment example does not reproduce.'
